  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nOur area has been upgraded into an Enhanced Risk (level 3 of 5) for \nsevere weather with strong to severe storms likely later today. \nToday's severe weather setup is becoming more clear and confidence \nis increasing as model guidance (especially recent runs of the HRRR) \npoints towards a strong MCS pushing through this afternoon and early \nevening. Radar and satellite trends over the past 6-12 hours show \nthat convection has been slow to develop overnight in Minnesota and \nWisconsin, which means timing has shifted towards the the\nafternoon into the evening for strong to severe storms in our\nforecast area. We continue to monitor ongoing thunderstorms \nover northeast Minnesota and northern Wisconsin, as this \nactivity should intensity as it propagates south/southeast along\nthe periphery of the upper level ridge into Michigan and \neventually northern Indiana and northwest Ohio by early to mid \nafternoon. There is still some uncertainty on exact track and \ntiming of storms but the best timing appears to be between 2 \nEDT to 8 PM EDT today. With a lack of storms in the area this \nmorning, this allows the airmass overhead ample time to heat up \nand destabilize before storms come through around peak diurnal \nheating. Highs are expected to be near 90s today with dewpoints \nin the 70s, which should fuel SBCAPE values of up to 2500-4000 \nJ/kg by early afternoon. Shear is expected to be around 30 to 40\nkts and mid level lapse rates look favorable around 7-8 C/km. \nThese are favorable parameters that will support a threat for \ndamaging winds of 60-70 mph. There is potential for significant \ngusts over 75 mph as CIG 1 hatching has been added for our \nentire forecast area by SPC in the new Day 1 outlook. An \nisolated brief tornado or two is also possible, especially with \nO-1 km SRH values of 150-250 m2/s2. \n\nAdditional scattered storms are possible after 8 PM along a diffuse \nfrontal boundary that tracks northwest to southeast, potentially \nclipping northeast Indiana and northwest Ohio. Have maintained NBM \nPoPs from 8 PM EDT tonight to 2 AM EDT Tuesday given considerable \nuncertainty. The severe threat should be much lower with these \nstorms given the likelihood for a stronger MCS earlier in the day as \nmentioned above.  \n\nThe good news is that after this evening, the threat for severe \nweather will be over and we will have several dry days ahead. A cold \nfront will sweep through Tuesday, setting the stage for several days \nwith highs in the mid 80s as high pressure builds over the Great \nLakes. It will be dry at least through Thursday with intermittent \nlow chances (20-30%) for rain/storm returning by Friday and into the \nweekend.\n\nAdditionally, as winds shift to become northwesterly on Tuesday, \ndangerous swimming conditions are expected to develop for southeast \nLake Michigan beaches on Tuesday. Waves of 3 to 5 feet Tuesday \nmorning will build to 7 to 10 feet by Tuesday afternoon/evening. \nWith direct onshore flow, strong structural, rip, and longshore \ncurrents are expected. Structural currents will be especially strong \non the north side of piers. Stay out of the water and stay off of \nthe piers! Beach Hazards Statements have been issued for La Porte \nCounty, IN and Berrien County, MI from early Tuesday through \ndaybreak Wednesday.",
